## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of batch processing with dynamic statistics and tight performance guarantees, blending order-statistics trees with buffering for amortization analysis.

## Key Concepts
- Order-statistics trees or multisets with subtree sizes and prefix sums
- Bulk-merge and batch amortization techniques
- Maintaining running sums to compute L1 deviations efficiently

## Code Example
```javascript
class TwoTierMedian {
  constructor() {
    this.committed = new OrderStatisticsTree();
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  insertBatch(batch) {
    this.buffer.push(...batch);
    this.buffer.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    this.committed.bulkMerge(this.buffer);
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  getMedian() {
    return this.committed.findByRank(Math.floor(this.committed.size / 2));
  }

  getSumAbsoluteDeviations() {
    const median = this.getMedian();
    return this.committed.sumAbsoluteDeviations(median);
  }
}
```

## Bulk-Merge and Rebalancing Strategy
Sort the buffer, then merge two sorted sequences into the OST using an in-order traversal. After merging, rebalance the tree (e.g., via treap randomization or AVL-style rotations) to maintain O(log n) operations.

## Amortized Per-Element Cost
Let B be the batch size. Sorting takes O(B log B), bulk-merge takes O(B), and rebalancing adds O(B). Amortized per element: O(log B) per batch element, which is optimal compared to O(log n) per individual insert.

## Memory Bounds
- OST stores all committed elements: O(n)
- Buffer stores one batch: O(B)
- Prefix sums at each node: O(n) additional memory

## Two Optimizations
1. **Lazy Buffering**: Accumulate multiple batches before merging to reduce merge frequency
2. **Hybrid Selection**: Use quickselect on the buffer first to find median bounds before querying the OST

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic skyline maintenance with batch updates, including amortized analysis and practical data-structure design.

## Key Concepts
- **2D dominance and Pareto frontier**: A point p dominates q if p.x ≥ q.x and p.y ≥ q.y with at least one strict inequality.
- **Invariant**: Frontier F is sorted by x with strictly increasing y, ensuring no two frontier points dominate each other.
- **Amortized analysis**: Each point is inserted once and removed at most once over n total points.
- **Space**: Uses O(n) space for all historical points plus O(|F|) for the current frontier.

## Data Structure
Use a balanced binary search tree (e.g., TreeMap) keyed by x-coordinate, with y-values stored alongside. This enables O(log |F|) insertion, O(1) access to the rightmost (largest x) point, and O(|F|) in-order traversal.

## Algorithm
1. Sort incoming batch by x-coordinate (O(B log B)).
2. Merge with frontier: for each batch point p, if p.y > current max frontier y, insert p and prune any frontier points with y ≤ p.y.
3. Enumerate frontier via in-order traversal.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Skyline {
  constructor() { this.frontier = new Map(); }

  insertBatch(points) {
    const sorted = points.sort((a, b) => a.x - b.x);
    let maxY = 0;

    for (const p of sorted) {
      if (p.y <= maxY) continue;

      for (const [x, y] of this.frontier) {
        if (y <= p.y) this.frontier.delete(x);
      }

      this.frontier.set(p.x, p.y);
      maxY = p.y;
    }
  }

  getFrontier() {
    return Array.from(this.frontier.entries())
      .sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Summary
| Operation | Cost |
|-----------|------|
| Batch insertion | O(B log B + |F|) amortized |
| Enumerate frontier | O(|F|) |
| Space | O(n) |

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design an incremental data structure for computing a non-trivial graph statistic under streaming batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental degree-based metrics
- Amortized analysis for batch/streaming updates
- Space-time trade-offs in dynamic graph algorithms

## Code Example
